        "WV-SD-11 is a heavily Republican district that elected Morris with 76% in the 2024 Seat B race, while Seat A went unopposed—a configuration that masks potential brittleness in the Republican coalition. The district's demographic profile is straightforward on the surface: 91.3% white, 44.9-year median age, 20.8% renter rate, and a $49,436 median household income that places constituents in the lower-middle income range for a state already facing economic stress. The 16.1% college-educated rate is notably low, indicating a constituency with limited educational credentials in an economy increasingly requiring them. Yet the 76% margin in a competitive cycle, combined with the unopposed Seat A result, suggests that Republican dominance here is built on cultural and partisan alignment rather than economic satisfaction—a distinction that matters strategically. The four reported demographic categories account for 93.8% of the population, leaving a 6.2% gap that in rural West Virginia likely represents American Indian or Alaska Native residents; while small in absolute numbers, this gap signals incomplete demographic accounting and potential for community concerns to surface if local governance fails.",
